tenant advocates are calling today for  the community to help families affected  by the pandemic they want leaders to  address evictions before the national  moratorium  expires at the end of this year hector  mejia has the story  well the group says they've heard  concerning stories of landlords trying  to go around the cdc moratorium  and intimidating tenants the  organization arriba las vegas  wants people to know that resources are  available they say  more help is needed especially for  immigrant workers  who are not eligible for unemployment  benefits  they estimate the number of people  facing imminent evictions  is in the thousands here in the valley  one of them is enrique gonzalez who has  not been able to return to work full  time as the only income earner in his  family  he says it's been extremely difficult to  pay the bills  since the moratorium here in las vegas  has ended  landlords feel like they have a green  light  that they can start using abusive  tactics to get tenants out  some of our members are resorting to  living in garages  living in their cars it is affecting  black and brown people low-income people  disproportionately  we are in a public safety crisis right  now they are struggling to inform people  about their rights  they describe this as a humanitarian  crisis  happening in our own backyard the  organization also believes a new wave of  economic hardship and homelessness could  plague the city  once the national moratorium expires on  december 31st  reporting near downtown hector mejia 8  news now
 

